---
/**
 * Run Viewer tool page: Railtracks runs, drawn by the visualizer from npm.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RunViewer from '../../components/tools/RunViewer';
import { href } from '../../lib/url';
---

<ToolLayout slug="run-viewer">
  <RunViewer client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="hud-card p-5">
        <h3 class="eyebrow text-accent mb-2">01 · The visualizer, live</h3>
        <p class="text-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
          The map is drawn by Railtracks' own visualizer, fetched from npm when the page opens. Pick
          the Railtracks version that wrote the log and the visualizer published alongside it is
          chosen for you, or pick a visualizer directly.
        </p>
        <div class="text-faint mt-3 font-mono text-[11px]">
          Both version lists are read from npm and PyPI on every visit.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hud-card p-5">
        <h3 class="eyebrow text-accent mb-2">02 · Either log format</h3>
        <p class="text-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Session files (<code>sessions/*.json</code>) open as written. Event streams (<code
            >events/*.jsonl</code
          >), which current Railtracks writes by default, are rebuilt into the same runs: one per
          top-level call, every tool and model call attached to the node that made it.
        </p>
        <div class="text-faint mt-3 font-mono text-[11px]">
          Checked against 210 sessions recorded in both formats: 199 identical.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hud-card p-5">
        <h3 class="eyebrow text-accent mb-2">03 · A folder, watched</h3>
        <p class="text-muted text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Open a project folder and every run in it is listed, newest first. In Chrome and Edge the
          folder stays open: new runs appear as your agent writes them, and a run in progress
          redraws as it grows.
        </p>
        <div class="text-faint mt-3 font-mono text-[11px]">
          Read access to that one folder, granted by you, revocable in the browser.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hud-card border-warn mt-6 border-l-2 p-5">
      <h3 class="eyebrow text-warn mb-3">Operational Limits & Assumptions</h3>
      <ul class="text-muted grid gap-3 text-xs leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-2">
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-accent font-mono font-bold">·</span>
          <span
            ><strong>Version matching is by date:</strong> a Railtracks release does not pin a visualizer,
            so the match is the newest one published on or before that release, which is what
            <code>railtracks viz</code> would have downloaded that day.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-accent font-mono font-bold">·</span>
          <span
            ><strong>V2 is not on npm yet:</strong> the event-stream visualizer behind
            <code>railtracks viz --beta</code> has not been published. It appears in the list on its own
            when it is, and the V1 visualizer draws both formats until then.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-accent font-mono font-bold">·</span>
          <span
            ><strong>Middleware and context are not drawn:</strong> the V1 map has no place for them,
            so those events are read but not shown. An event stream missing lines (a crash mid-write)
            draws what survived.</span
          >
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2">
          <span class="text-accent font-mono font-bold">·</span>
          <span
            ><strong>Sandboxed, and restyled from outside:</strong> the visualizer runs in a frame with
            no access to this site. The Site style recolours it through its own theme and a layer of CSS,
            so a future build may draw parts of itself in its original colours.</span
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
      Railtracks is the agent framework I designed at Railtown AI. The <a
        href={href('/blog/introducing-railtracks/')}>introduction</a
      > covers what it records and why.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
